  "metadata": {
    "title": "[SYNTHETIC FIXTURE] Hexylphosphonic acid passivation of CsPbBr3 nanocube surfaces",
    "authors": [],
    "doi": null,
    "year": null,
    "summary": "Synthetic fixture for pipeline testing, not a real publication: it asks how hexylphosphonic acid (HPA) treatment raises the photoluminescence quantum yield of CsPbBr3 nanocubes. XRD indexes the nanocubes to cubic CsPbBr3 with a = 5.87 A, and TEM shows ~10 nm cubes bounded by {100} facets. After treatment the FTIR P-OH band at 950 cm-1 disappears and the P=O band shifts from 1200 to 1100 cm-1, the XPS Br/Pb atomic ratio drops from 2.9 to 2.6 while a P 2p peak appears at 133.2 eV, and the PL quantum yield increases from 60% to 90%. The authors conclude that HPA binds as a deprotonated phosphonate to surface Pb, taking the place of surface bromide, and that removing undercoordinated surface Pb sites suppresses non-radiative recombination. The reconstruction target is the phosphonate-passivated CsPbBr3 {100} surface; monodentate versus bidentate binding, the degree of deprotonation (one or two P-OH groups), CsBr- versus PbBr2-terminated facets and the ligand surface coverage are unresolved."
  },
